      "originalText": "Introduction to Autowiring\n========================\n\n1. One of the key faetures of Spring framework is dependency injection, which make sit easy to manage dependencies between objects .\n\n2. But till now in every example , we were explicitly coding this dependecy to a certain extent .\n\n3. It works but still requres dependecies to be managed by us .\n\n4. This is where Autowiring comes into picture .\n\n5. Autowiring is a mechanism is Pring that allows the framework to automatically detect and inject the dependent beans to properties of the target bean without the need for explicit configuration .\n\n6. In simple words, when we apply autowiring , then Spring looks for the dependent beans int the container and automatically injects them into the target object .\n\n\n\nbenefit\n---------\n\nThis can save a lot of time and efforts , especially in large sacle applications where there are large number of dependecies between objects .\n\n\nDrawbacks\n--------------\n\nNo control of the programmer .\n\nIt can't be used for primitive .\n\n\nSyntax\n---------\n\nTo add autowiring support in our code we have to use the autowire attribute in <bean> tag in XML configuration file .\n\n<bean id = \"  \" class = \" \" autowire = \" \" />\n\nautowiring modes\n---------------------\n\n1. no ->This mode tells the framework that autowiring is not to be done . It is the default mode used by Spring .\n\n2. byName -> It uses the name of bean for injecting dependencies .\n\n3. byType -> It injects the dependecy according to the type of bean .\n\n4. constructor -> It injects the required depedencies by invoking the constructor .\n\n5. autodetect -> deprecated from Spring 3.0 onwards .\n\n\n\nSetting Autowire to byName  --> $1Autowiring-1\n-----------------------------------\n\n-> This mode uses the name of bean for injecting dependencies .\n\n-> However , it requires that the name of the property/data member in the target bean must be same as bean id in the xml configuration .\n\n-. It internally invoke the setter method for autowiring .\n\n-> if no matching is found property will remain unwired .\n\n\nSetting autowire to byType --> $2Autowiring-2\n-----------------------------------\n\n-> This mode injects the dependency according to the type of the bean .\n\n-> It looks up in the configuration file for class type of the property .\n\n-> If it finds a unique bean that matches , it injects the property .\n\n-> It internally invokes the setter method for autowiring .\n\n-> If their are multiple beans of same type then it throws NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException .\n\n-> Solving Ambiguity problem : --> $3Autowiring-3\n\n  -> To handle this and help Spring select a particular bean to be injected we can set an attribute called autowire-candidate to false for all other beans .\n\n-> This means that for all the beans where autowire-candidate is set to false , the spring container will not consider them for autowiring .\n\n\n# Spring Framework – Primary vs Autowire Candidate\n\n## 1. Why do we use `primary`? --> $4Autowiring-4\n\nWhen multiple beans of the same type are available, Spring may get confused about which bean to inject using autowiring.\n\nTo resolve this ambiguity, we use `primary=\"true\"`.\n\n### Example:\n\n```xml\n<bean id=\"hpLaptop\"\n      class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"\n      primary=\"true\"/>\n\n<bean id=\"dellLaptop\"\n      class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"/>\n\n<bean id=\"lenovoLaptop\"\n      class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"/>\n```\n\nHere, all three beans are eligible for autowiring, but Spring gives preference to `hpLaptop` when resolving a single dependency.\n\n**Meaning:** `primary=\"true\"` marks a bean as the preferred autowiring candidate.\n\n## 2. What is `autowire-candidate=\"false\"`?\n\nIt tells Spring not to consider a particular bean as a candidate for autowiring.\n\n### Example:\n\n```xml\n<bean id=\"hpLaptop\"\n      class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"\n      autowire-candidate=\"false\"/>\n\n<bean id=\"dellLaptop\"\n      class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"/>\n\n<bean id=\"lenovoLaptop\"\n      class=\"in.spring.beans.Laptop\"/>\n```\n\nHere:\n- HP is excluded from autowiring.\n- Dell and Lenovo remain eligible.\n- Spring can select Dell or Lenovo for autowiring.\n\n**Meaning:** `autowire-candidate=\"false\"` excludes a bean from autowiring candidate selection.\n\n## 3. Difference between Primary and Autowire Candidate\n\n| Primary | Autowire Candidate False |\n|---|---|\n| Gives preference to a bean | Excludes a bean |\n| Bean remains eligible for autowiring | Bean is not eligible for autowiring |\n| Other beans also remain eligible | Other beans can be selected instead |\n| Used when one bean should be preferred | Used when a bean should not be considered |\n\n## 4. Important Points\n\n1. `primary=\"true\"` is used to resolve ambiguity when multiple beans of the same type are available.\n2. Only one primary bean should be configured for a given injection type in the same candidate set.\n3. `autowire-candidate=\"false\"` removes a bean from autowiring candidate selection.\n4. It does not prevent explicit bean injection using `ref`.\n5. Both attributes affect autowiring, not whether Spring creates the bean.\n\n## 5. Interview Answer\n\n**Q. What is the difference between primary and autowire-candidate?**\n\n`primary=\"true\"` gives preference to a particular bean when multiple candidates are available for autowiring, whereas `autowire-candidate=\"false\"` excludes a bean from autowiring candidate selection.\n\n**Remember:**\n\n- Primary = Prefer this bean.\n- Autowire candidate false = Don't consider this bean for autowiring.\n\nSetting autowire to constructor --> $5Autowiring-5   --> $6Autowiring-6\n-----------------------------------------                       \n\n-> This mode works only for constructor injection .\n\n-> In this Spring tries to match up the bean first by parameter name defined in the target class constructor' s defined in the target class constructor's arguement list and if it couldn't find a match then by class name of the dependent bean .",
